{"version":"1","id":"mattrickard","handle":"mattrickard","name":"Matt Rickard","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/t_logo,f_auto/v1/logos/ab45899954994f6ba0d5ede282d3f27a","description":null,"is_squad":false,"x":-3.2848,"y":3.2075,"cluster_id":3,"posts_collected":235,"newest_post_at":"2026-04-01T04:14:51.942Z","oldest_post_at":"2023-02-23T13:00:21.909Z","features":{"hype_score":0.002128,"listicle_ratio":0.004255,"question_ratio":0.068085,"avg_read_time":1.723404,"summary_length_avg":279.587234,"title_length_avg":28.165957,"title_length_var":116.793735,"tag_entropy":4.49604,"tag_diversity":0.651064,"top_tag_share":0.106383,"avg_upvotes":2.723404,"median_upvotes":1.0,"avg_comments":0.212766,"comment_to_upvote_ratio":0.079563,"zero_engagement_share":0.4,"viral_share":0.004255,"posts_per_week":1.452365,"non_article_ratio":0.0,"author_present_share":0.0,"recency_skew":0.004274},"view_coords":{"engagement_style":[1.2794,7.1765],"cadence_depth":[3.6912,-8.0441],"hype_variety":[-3.1324,9.1912],"title_style":[7.2941,0.8824]},"top_tags":[["ai",35],["open-source",16],["startup",16],["cloud",15],["infrastructure",14],["openai",13],["google",11],["kubernetes",11]],"sample_titles":{"representative":["Why Tailwind CSS Won","Why Fast?","TypeScript Type System Hacks"],"outlier":["React LLM: Easily Run Models in the Browser with Headless Components"]},"recent_posts":[{"id":"Egj5Dj0VQ","title":"The Spec Layer","url":"https://mattrickard.com/the-spec-layer","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/the-spec-layer-egj5dj0vq","summary":"AI agents fail differently than humans \u2014 they don't break builds, they disable failing tests, reuse nearest patterns, and make locally valid but globally wrong decisions. The root cause is underconstrained execution: too much freedom at the point of action. Spec-driven development (SDD) is proposed as a solution: writing durable intent before implementation to constrain what agents can decide on their own. Drawing on protocol engineering (RFCs, TLS, HTML specs) and formal methods (TLA+, Dijkstra), the post argues for a layered model where specs constrain intent, plans constrain approach, tasks constrain sequencing, and tests/lint constrain behavior. Tools like GitHub Spec Kit, Kiro, OpenSpec, and Augment Intent are surveyed. The ideal spec is declarative, layered, and cheap to revise \u2014 with mechanical rules pushed into lint and tests rather than prose. The goal: smaller specs, harder checks, less agent guessing.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/b56cd664de40c09f421fe464fcd62230?_a=AQAEuop","num_upvotes":7,"num_comments":2,"created_at":"2026-04-01T04:14:51.942Z"},{"id":"fare4a94D","title":"Using Claude Code from Anywhere","url":"https://mattrickard.com/claude-code-anywhere","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/using-claude-code-from-anywhere-fare4a94d","summary":"A developer shares their frustrations with managing multiple Claude Code instances and presents a new workflow solution. The approach uses ephemeral sandboxes with web UI access, enabling live interactive sessions that can be managed from anywhere, not just laptops. The solution addresses parallelism overhead, mobility limitations, and provides automated branch management with pull request creation.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/266c0a42d2ae5d3d774dffe63591ad73?_a=AQAEulh","num_upvotes":1,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2025-08-30T20:11:14.482Z"},{"id":"XrwwAn4om","title":"Static Sites Aren't Simple Anymore","url":"https://matt-rickard.com/static-sites-arent-simple-anymore","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/static-sites-aren-t-simple-anymore-xrwwan4om","summary":"Static sites have become more complex and require fast page loads, easy writing, dynamic capabilities, interactivity with JavaScript, easy design, and no infrastructure management.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/c92bfecd44bdc5e824f8fa34e4798185?_a=AQAEufR","num_upvotes":6,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2023-11-04T13:30:20.000Z"},{"id":"mrXFZ9AU6","title":"Lessons from llama.cpp","url":"https://matt-rickard.com/lessons-from-llama-cpp","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/lessons-from-llama-cpp-mrxfz9au6","summary":"Llama.cpp is an active open-source community that implements Meta's LLaMA architecture in C/C++. It runs everywhere, serves as a Schelling point for low-level features, has a custom model format, and focuses on a single model architecture.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/73b7e6c105e0d12cf2e35b1f0d88fa40?_a=AQAEufR","num_upvotes":2,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2023-11-03T06:30:25.000Z"},{"id":"pEnqOON7f","title":"Mechanical Turks","url":"https://matt-rickard.com/mechanical-turks","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/mechanical-turks-penqoon7f","summary":"The Mechanical Turk was a chess-playing AI constructed in 1770 that cleverly concealed a human operator inside. In 2005, Amazon launched its own version of the Mechanical Turk called Amazon Mechanical Turk.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/d15b769740094670ce2e85f96f99c5b7?_a=AQAEufR","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2023-11-01T06:30:41.000Z"},{"id":"A1hQzLcJx","title":"Regulatory Capture in the Railroad Industry","url":"https://matt-rickard.com/regulatory-capture-in-the-railroad-industry","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/regulatory-capture-in-the-railroad-industry-a1hqzlcjx","summary":"The regulatory agency, ICC, in the railroad industry ended up protecting the interests of the industry it was supposed to regulate. The rate setting process disproportionately favored railroad companies, there were barriers to entry for competitors, industry influence within the ICC, and complex rules benefited established companies.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/58be3da868203c497de382a2b189b029?_a=AQAEufR","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2023-10-31T06:30:52.000Z"},{"id":"D5YcxUkmZ","title":"What If OpenAI Builds This?","url":"https://matt-rickard.com/what-if-openai-builds-this","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/what-if-openai-builds-this--d5ycxukmz","summary":"Open AI has released an update to ChatGPT that enables users to 'chat' with PDF documents. This update may lead to increased competition and lower margins in the market for similar products. Large companies like OpenAI face unique challenges when entering new markets.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/34c145932b80352c785fe019458f1673?_a=AQAEufR","num_upvotes":4,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2023-10-30T06:30:12.000Z"},{"id":"g7yzG4T78","title":"On Mixing Client and Server","url":"https://matt-rickard.com/on-mixing-client-and-server","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/on-mixing-client-and-server-g7yzg4t78","summary":"Mixing client and server code in React with Server Components allows for more performant web applications, colocated code for quicker iteration, and React Component as the new API. However, it can also cause confusion in determining whether a component is a client or server component, impact dependencies and code organization, complicate the render pipeline, and introduce implicit infrastructure assumptions.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/3dfa02c1abd7ca2064ac7630ca8191fc?_a=AQAEufR","num_upvotes":12,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2023-10-29T06:30:33.000Z"},{"id":"ppUwrwOSV","title":"Infrastructure as Code Will be Written by AI","url":"https://matt-rickard.com/infrastructure-as-code-will-be-written-by-ai","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/infrastructure-as-code-will-be-written-by-ai-ppuwrwosv","summary":"AI will write the majority of infrastructure-as-code, infer infrastructure components for application code, convert infrastructure between providers, help developers write their own IaC, and suggest changesets for complicated templates.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/890aab0d321ad3e9967184db16f7c230?_a=AQAEufR","num_upvotes":3,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2023-10-28T06:30:21.000Z"},{"id":"Hd4vvGJrq","title":"When A/B Testing Doesn't Work","url":"https://matt-rickard.com/when-a-b-testing-doesnt-work","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/when-a-b-testing-doesn-t-work-hd4vvgjrq","summary":"A/B testing may not be effective for most products as they often lack sufficient data, lead to incremental changes, require double the work, and may not have clear goals. Startups, in particular, need to prioritize and may not have the resources for extensive testing.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/3238cf34af079cf496ad992d0aa3807a?_a=AQAEufR","num_upvotes":2,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2023-10-27T06:30:09.000Z"}],"similar_handles":["aspnet","thisdot","cybertec_postgresql","appsignal","finout"],"opposite_handles":["felixrieseberg","hamelhusain","xai","hwchase17","sama"],"similar":[{"id":"aspnet","handle":"aspnet","name":"ASP.NET Blog","is_squad":false,"x":-3.3839,"y":3.0543,"cluster_id":3,"posts_collected":228,"image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/t_logo,f_auto/v1/logos/ff70247f4b29446e9d30433c3f38652a","features_preview":{"posts_per_week":0.653569,"median_upvotes":1.0,"author_present_share":0.0,"hype_score":0.039474,"avg_read_time":8.552632,"tag_diversity":0.473684},"top_tags_preview":[[".net",145],["blazor",44],["aspnet",40]],"view_coords":{"engagement_style":[4.8382,-3.8088],"cadence_depth":[3.5735,4.9412],"hype_variety":[7.6176,5.7941],"title_style":[0.1765,-6.7059]}},{"id":"thisdot","handle":"thisdot","name":"This Dot","is_squad":false,"x":-3.3477,"y":3.43,"cluster_id":3,"posts_collected":509,"image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/t_logo,f_auto/v1/logos/6e22565142ee45b386c8bc12bdb3d117","features_preview":